 :notes
 [  "Post-group-5 keyset trade, recorded deliberately: a RAW (storeless)
   first-50 page whose result exceeds the page now materializes the
   sorted closure per page (probe-then-continue), trading the old
   streaming page for correctness (no skip/dup) and total-walk cost:
   client walks pay the closure ONCE then slice zero-scan pages, and
   raw small-result reads (the 0tx lookup-subjects shape) keep probe
   early-stop economics. Raw deep pagination of large recursive results
   should attach a client - this is the documented remediation, not an
   open bug. Counts and point checks are unchanged by group 5."
    "Recording methodology changed at post-group-4: medians now come from
   the canonical gate test on a FRESH JVM (scenarios sequential, star-2k
   first and therefore JIT-coldest — its apparent regression vs the
   post-group-3 warm-JVM numbers is harness state, not code; the warmed
   10k/chain scenarios improved ~5-8% from the boundary marshalling)."
  "Every recorded ratio exceeds the 2.0 release bound — this is the
   audited populated-recursion regression (0tx report corroborated on a
   matched harness), pre-fix by construction: groups 3 (raw request
   context), 4 (kernel-boundary phase 1), and 5 (keyset recursion)
   land against these numbers and flip :status to :enforced as they
   tighten."
  "star-10k can-deep v7 median (0.016ms) is faster than star-2k's
   (0.066ms) because the v7 JVM was hotter by the later run; medians
   are comparative, not portable — the variance policy and logical-work
   authority govern."
  "chain-10k :can-deep is the recorded v8 robustness win: v7's
   recursive evaluation stack-overflows at ~7.5k depth; v8's
   fuel-bounded iterative generated machine completes in 366ms."]}
